      "message": "Eck, yes, probably? Ideally, every user defined setting should work by being set by the build `-DSK_USE_THING\u003dBAR`, or by their `SkUserConfig.h` like `#define SK_USE_THING BAR` (it is their build, so they can deal with any conflicts here, with our default `SkUserConfig.h` not defining anything), and then Skia can set a default\n```\n#if !defined(SK_USE_THING)\n#  define SK_USE_THING FOO\n#endif\n```\n\nThough you are right, we\u0027ve not been very good at adding commented out \"not defines\" in our `SkUserConfig.h\" to document these.\n\nI suppose this could also be said for `SkAssert.h` which should directly include `SkUserConfig.h` because it could be used to define `SK_ABORT`. The issue here of course is that we want our `SkUserConfig.h` to never define anything, so it should never look like `SkUserConfig.h` is ever used to iwyu. Maybe the solution to this is to have an alternate `SkUserConfig.h` for iwyu builds which defines everything it possibly can. This might also help us notice when a new configuration macro is added but hasn\u0027t been added to `SkUserConfig.h` (or at least the iwyu one, which would have a note about being sure to add to the \"empty\" one). Then we wouldn\u0027t need all these keep pragmas.",
